  exp: "The patient is experiencing UNSTABLE Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) but still HAS A PULSE. Because he is hemodynamically unstable (hypotension, confusion, diaphoresis), immediate electrical therapy is required. The correct intervention for unstable tachyarrhythmias WITH A PULSE is Synchronized Cardioversion. The defibrillator synchronizes the shock delivery to the R wave of the QRS complex to avoid shocking during the vulnerable relative refractory period (T wave), which could precipitate Ventricular Fibrillation.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Unsynchronized defibrillation):</strong> Defibrillation is strictly reserved for PULSELESS rhythms (Ventricular Fibrillation or Pulseless VT). Shocking a patient with a pulse unsynchronized can cause the shock to land on the T-wave (R-on-T phenomenon), triggering VF.<br>• <strong>Option C (Transcutaneous pacing):</strong> Pacing is the treatment for unstable symptomatic bradycardias (e.g., third-degree AV block), not tachycardias.<br>• <strong>Option D (Precordial thump):</strong> Precordial thump is an obsolete, rarely effective maneuver historically considered for witnessed, monitored pulseless VT/VF without a defibrillator immediately available."
