  ans: 1,
  exp: "According to the Surviving Sepsis Campaign guidelines, the immediate cornerstone of resuscitation for sepsis-induced hypoperfusion (hypotension or lactate >= 4 mmol/L) is the rapid administration of at least 30 mL/kg of intravenous crystalloids (e.g., Normal Saline or Ringer's Lactate). This initial fluid bolus should be completed within the first 3 hours of presentation to restore intravascular volume and improve tissue perfusion.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (500 mL of 5% Albumin):</strong> Albumin is considered a second-line fluid if patients require massive amounts of crystalloids, but is not the initial standard therapy.<br>• <strong>Option C (10 mL/kg of hypertonic saline):</strong> Hypertonic saline is used for severe hyponatremia or elevated intracranial pressure, not for primary sepsis resuscitation.<br>• <strong>Option D (Immediate initiation of Norepinephrine):</strong> Vasopressors (Norepinephrine is first-line) are initiated only if hypotension persists DURING or AFTER adequate fluid resuscitation, not before."
