  q: "A 22-year-old male is admitted to the ICU after a motorcycle collision resulting in a complete spinal cord transection at the level of C5. He is hypotensive (BP 75/40 mmHg) but his extremities are warm and well-perfused. His heart rate is 45 bpm. What is the primary pathophysiological mechanism causing this specific hemodynamic profile?",
  opts: ["Massive hemorrhage causing loss of intravascular volume", "Loss of descending sympathetic tone leading to extreme vasodilation and unopposed vagal tone", "Systemic inflammatory response to tissue trauma causing profound capillary leak", "Direct myocardial contusion impairing left ventricular contractility"],
  ans: 1,
  exp: "The patient is in Neurogenic Shock, a form of distributive shock occurring after high spinal cord injury (typically above T6). The transection disrupts the descending sympathetic pathways from the brainstem to the thoracolumbar spinal cord. This causes a sudden loss of sympathetic vasomotor tone, resulting in massive peripheral vasodilation (warm, flushed extremities and hypotension). Furthermore, the loss of cardiac sympathetic innervation (T1-T4) leaves parasympathetic (vagal) tone unopposed, classically causing bradycardia despite the severe hypotension (unlike hypovolemic shock, which causes tachycardia).",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Massive hemorrhage):</strong> Hypovolemic shock presents with cold, clammy, pale extremities (due to intense compensatory sympathetic vasoconstriction) and tachycardia.<br>• <strong>Option C (Systemic inflammatory response):</strong> This describes septic or severe SIRS shock. While it causes warm extremities (distributive), it invariably causes tachycardia, not bradycardia.<br>• <strong>Option D (Direct myocardial contusion):</strong> Cardiogenic shock presents with poor perfusion, cold extremities, elevated central venous pressure, and usually tachycardia or complex arrhythmias."
