  opts: ["High tidal volumes (10-12 mL/kg predicted body weight) to recruit collapsed alveoli", "Low tidal volumes (6 mL/kg predicted body weight) to limit plateau pressure", "Zero positive end-expiratory pressure (ZEEP) to prevent barotrauma", "Permissive hyperoxemia (PaO2 > 150 mmHg) to ensure tissue oxygen delivery"],
  ans: 1,
  exp: "The hallmark of mechanical ventilation in ARDS is 'Lung-Protective Ventilation'. The ARDSNet protocol demonstrated a significant mortality benefit by using low tidal volumes (calculated at 6 mL/kg of predicted body weight) to prevent overdistension (volutrauma). The goal is to keep the plateau pressure (Pplat) ≤ 30 cm H2O. This strategy often results in 'permissive hypercapnia', which is generally well tolerated.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (High tidal volumes):</strong> High tidal volumes cause volutrauma and barotrauma to the fragile, heterogeneous ARDS lung, increasing mortality.<br>• <strong>Option C (Zero PEEP):</strong> Adequate PEEP is essential in ARDS to prevent cyclic alveolar collapse and reopening (atelectrauma) and to improve oxygenation.<br>• <strong>Option D (Permissive hyperoxemia):</strong> High oxygen concentrations lead to oxygen toxicity and absorption atelectasis; the goal is usually a conservative PaO2 (55-80 mmHg) or SpO2 (88-95%)."
