  q: "A trauma patient receives a massive blood transfusion of 12 units of packed red blood cells over 2 hours during damage control surgery. The anesthesiologist notices prolonged QT intervals on the ECG and hypotension that is unresponsive to fluids. What metabolic complication of massive transfusion is most likely responsible for these findings?",
  opts: ["Hypercalcemia due to RBC lysis", "Hypocalcemia due to citrate toxicity", "Hypokalemia due to cellular shift", "Hypernatremia due to saline preservatives"],
  ans: 1,
  exp: "Massive blood transfusion can cause significant metabolic derangements. Banked blood contains citrate as an anticoagulant (CPDA-1). When transfused rapidly, the liver cannot metabolize the citrate fast enough. The excess citrate binds (chelates) ionized calcium in the patient's plasma, causing acute, severe hypocalcemia. Hypocalcemia presents with hypotension, decreased myocardial contractility, and prolonged QT intervals on the ECG. It is treated with intravenous calcium gluconate or calcium chloride.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Hypercalcemia):</strong> Massive transfusion causes hypocalcemia, not hypercalcemia.<br>• <strong>Option C (Hypokalemia):</strong> Massive transfusion of stored blood typically causes HYPERkalemia, because red blood cells slowly leak potassium during storage.<br>• <strong>Option D (Hypernatremia):</strong> While blood products contain sodium, hypernatremia is rarely the cause of acute hypotension and prolonged QT intervals during massive transfusion."
